no harmful ingress.” That’s a minimum warranty. It absolutely does **not** mean that at 31 minutes a timer goes off and water rushes in. The seals might hold for hours in calm conditions. But the manufacturer refuses to *guarantee* that, because once you go beyond the tested window, the failure rate becomes unpredictable. Think of it like a best-before date on food — the yogurt doesn’t instantly spoil the next day, but the producer won’t vouch for it anymore. ### 4. Real world vs. lab settings The lake added variables the rating never accounted for: silt, microbes, water contaminants that can accelerate seal degradation, and varying pressure as your buddy dived down to retrieve it. The fact that it survived suggests the lake was relatively benign. But other people have dropped the same phone model in a similar lake and ended up with waterlogged devices because one tiny rubber gasket had a grain of sand on it, or the phone was moved through the water in a way that momentarily exceeded the pressure limit. The time limit exists because beyond 30 minutes, the statistical probability of seal failure climbs to a level that the manufacturer no longer accepts as safe. ### 5. “Waterproof” is a marketing slipperiness, not an engineering term No reputable phone maker today calls a device “waterproof.” They all use “water-resistant.” That’s not just legal cover — it reflects the reality that all seals are temporary barriers, not permanent walls. Even a rugged dive watch is only “waterproof” up to a rated static pressure, and its gaskets need changing every few years. A phone’s seals can’t be serviced; they age with heat, drops, and pocket lint. So the rating is a snapshot of factory-fresh capability under idealized conditions. ### So what’s the takeaway? Your buddy’s experience doesn’t contradict the existence of a time limit — it just shows that the limit is a **conservative warranty boundary**, not a physical cliff. He got to enjoy the bonus of a device that held up beyond it